  "question": {
    "text": "If the value of $\\frac{3 \\cos 36^{\\circ}+5 \\sin 18^{\\circ}}{5 \\cos 36^{\\circ}-3 \\sin 18^{\\circ}}$ is $\\frac{a \\sqrt{5}-b}{c}$, where $a, b, c$ are natural numbers and $\\operatorname{gcd}(a, c)=1$, then $a+b+c$ is equal to :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "0KEla",
      "text": "40",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "yCjeo",
      "text": "52",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ffCur",
      "text": "50",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "TklLH",
      "text": "54",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{\\frac{3(\\sqrt{5}+1)}{4}+5\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1}{4}\\right)}{5\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1}{4}\\right)-3\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1}{4}\\right)}=\\frac{8 \\sqrt{5}-2}{2 \\sqrt{5}+8} \\\\ & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}-1}{\\sqrt{5}+4} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{5}-4}{\\sqrt{5}-4}\\end{aligned}$\n$\\begin{aligned} & =\\frac{20-16 \\sqrt{5}-\\sqrt{5}+4}{-11} \\\\ & =\\frac{17 \\sqrt{5}-24}{11} \\Rightarrow a=17, b=27, c=11 \\\\ & a+b+c=52\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
